---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionIntro from "../../components/SectionIntro.astro";
import { recipeCards } from "../../data/content";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Food Therapy | HerbGuide"
  description="Practical Chinese kitchen ideas with simple soups, teas, pantry basics, and beginner-friendly routines."
  structuredData={[
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "CollectionPage",
      name: "Food Therapy",
      description:
        "Practical Chinese kitchen ideas with simple soups, teas, pantry basics, and beginner-friendly routines.",
      url: "https://herbguide.store/food-therapy/",
    },
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is Chinese food therapy the same as taking herbs like medicine?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not necessarily. This section focuses on ingredient-level use such as soups, teas, porridges, and seasonal cooking.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What are the easiest Chinese food therapy ingredients to start with?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Ginger, jujube, goji berry, chrysanthemum, mint, black sesame, lotus seed, and pear are among the easiest beginner-friendly ingredients.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Food Therapy</p>
      <h1>Simple recipes and gentle routines</h1>
      <p>
        This section keeps TCM practical through soups, teas, pantry guides, and seasonal
        kitchen ideas that are easier to try than dense theory.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionIntro
        title="Starter topics"
        text="The launch site focuses on beginner-friendly recipe content with familiar ingredients, cultural context, and careful safety language."
      />
      <div class="stack">
        {recipeCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="list-card">
            <h2>{card.title}</h2>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">What food therapy is</p>
        <h2>Kitchen guidance, not treatment advice</h2>
        <p>
          Chinese food therapy sits at the meeting point of household cooking,
          seasonality, ingredient pairing, and traditional theory. It usually asks simple
          questions: is the weather dry or damp, cold or hot, light or heavy, and what
          kinds of everyday foods are commonly used in that setting?
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why this section focuses on teas, soups, porridge, pantry ingredients, and
          ingredient pairings. It is one of the easiest entry points for many readers,
          especially in American English educational content.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">How to use this section</p>
        <h2>Start with familiar ingredients</h2>
        <p>
          Beginner-friendly food therapy works best when the ingredients already feel
          recognizable. Ginger, jujube, goji berry, chrysanthemum, mint, pear, black sesame,
          and lotus seed are easier to trust than obscure concentrated products.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Choose simple recipes with one clear theme.</li>
          <li>Prefer food-level use over concentrated self-directed use.</li>
          <li>Watch the cautions on individual herb pages.</li>
          <li>Use repeated gentle learning instead of exaggerated promises.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Traditional Chinese food therapy for beginners</h2>
        <p>
          A warming soup article might center ginger, jujube, and astragalus in cold-weather
          cooking. A cooling tea article might introduce chrysanthemum or mint with simple
          language around traditional heat. A nourishing pantry article might explain why
          goji berry, black sesame, longan, or lotus seed show up so often in home recipes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal is to make the site useful in the kitchen first. That creates a more
          trustworthy editorial tone than jumping immediately into claim-heavy language.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Chinese medicinal food recipes without hype</h2>
        <p>
          Food therapy can sound gentle, but it still deserves boundaries. HerbGuide will
          not use recipe content to imply treatment of disease, replacement of medications,
          or personalized instructions for pregnancy, chronic conditions, or ongoing concerns.
        </p>
        <p>
          Readers should come away with cultural understanding, practical ingredient ideas,
          and a better vocabulary for reading TCM content online.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Long-tail topics</p>
        <h2>Best Chinese food therapy ingredients for everyday cooking</h2>
        <p>
          One of the most useful long-term goals for this section is to answer very specific
          search intent in plain English. That includes topics like best Chinese food therapy
          ingredients for beginners, easy warming soup ingredients for cold weather, and how
          to use goji berry, ginger, jujube, or chrysanthemum in everyday kitchen routines.
        </p>
        <p>
          These are the kinds of search phrases real readers use when they are curious but
          still cautious. They fit HerbGuide better than aggressive claim-driven lifestyle
          language.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Depth plan</p>
        <h2>Chinese medicinal soup recipes with step-by-step instructions</h2>
        <p>
          As this section expands, the strongest premium-style topics are detailed Chinese
          traditional soup recipes, herbal tea recipes with step-by-step instructions, and
          simple weekly meal structures built around season, comfort, and pantry-friendly
          ingredients.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each page can start with a clear ingredient list, explain why those ingredients are
          traditionally paired, and then walk through timing, simmering, preparation, and
          practical serving ideas.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reader questions</p>
        <h2>Food therapy FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Is Chinese food therapy the same as taking herbs like medicine?</h3>
        <p>
          Not necessarily. Food therapy usually begins with ingredient-level use such as
          soups, teas, porridges, and seasonal cooking. It sits closer to the kitchen than
          to concentrated herbal use.
        </p>
        <h3>What are the easiest Chinese food therapy ingredients to start with?</h3>
        <p>
          Ginger, jujube, goji berry, chrysanthemum, mint, black sesame, lotus seed, and
          pear are among the easiest to explain because they already feel close to food.
        </p>
        <h3>Will HerbGuide include full recipe steps later?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes. The strongest next-stage pages for this section are detailed soups, teas, and
          ingredient pairings with exact preparation steps and practical serving notes.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</p>
        <h2>Best next reads for this section</h2>
        <p>
          If you want food-friendly herbs first, start with the herb profiles for
          <a href="/herbs/goji-berry/">Goji Berry</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/fresh-ginger/">Fresh Ginger</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/jujube-red-date/">Jujube</a>, and
          <a href="/herbs/chrysanthemum-flower/">Chrysanthemum</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want the theory behind warming, cooling, and seasonal ingredient choices,
          the next stop should be <a href="/basics/">TCM Basics</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
